@back@
.Sh NAME
.Nm Alex G - New
.Sh DESCRIPTION
I've been getting into Alex G recently, because there's this guy on the
internet,
.Lk https://youtu.be/amKk-e6Ee9w Strumdoc ,
who does covers of a lot of his songs. I found Strumdoc by stumbling upon his
Radiohead covers.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
[Verse 1]
.Do Bobby drives all alone
To the park by the creek
And he meets up with Jan
Passed the bill, shakes her hand
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
This is my first time writing my thoughts about a song. I think that I think
quite a bit about song lyrics compared to most people. It's been a few times
now that I've brought up my interpretation or my feelings about a song, and
friends have been surprised that I've thought about the meaning of the words at
all. This is a pretty depressing song to start with. On brand for me I suppose.
The song is obviously about drugs. Bobby is an addict, and Jan is his dealer.
.Pp
I've noticed that many people can't understand substance dependency. They see
how much destruction substance abuse can bring to an addict's life, their
relationships. It's fairly ironic when 2/3rds of the world are addicted to
coffee, and such a large number of people's alcohol habits would be medically
classified as "binge drinking." But I guess most people keep their vices
moderate. In only four brief lines, we know Bobby is an addict, and I think
.Dq all alone
speaks that he's probably quite isolated.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
.Do Jan got home after ten
Woke her dad, went to bed
Woke up cold in the night
From a voice in her head
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
I find it surprising that Alex paints Jan's life first. She lives at home, and
she wakes her dad when she gets in. I think her dad is mentioned on purpose,
and there's a few things that it makes me assume. Her mum isn't mentioned,
which makes me think that her parents are separated. Her dad is already asleep
by ten, and he's asleep before Jan gets back home. It doesn't seem like he is
worried about her being out late. I have to assume he wasn't pleased to be
woken up.
.Pp
Jan wakes up at night to a voice in her head. It makes me think of the phrase
.Dq how do you sleep at night?
Jan wakes up cold, like the words the voice spoke weighed heavy on her heart,
which are given in the chorus.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
[Chorus]
.Do Who are you?
I guess it's what we do
To be new
What a scheme
I guess that's what she means
In your dreams
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
The chorus in the song seems to serve two purposes. The first chorus gives the
words that keep Jan up at night out of guilt. I read it as two voices.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
[Voice]
Who are you?
[Jan]
I guess it's what we do to be new.
[Voice]
What a scheme.
.Ed
.Pp
I think the voice is Jan's old self, struggling to recognize herself now. She
asks herself how she can justify taking advantage of Bobby's addiction for
money. Jan rationalizes it, and tells herself it's just what she is doing to
support a new life. Maybe she needs out of her family life. The voice replies
with a value judgement, calling it a scheme, along with its negative
connotation.
.Pp
.Dq It's what we do
can also be read like, 
.Dq it's what people do
to be new. I think it kinda is what people do. There are so many examples of
exploitation in modern society. Gambling companies. Corner stores selling
disposable vapes. Software companies that make a profit out of warfare. Stocks
& Shares with companies that profit from all of the above. Often the everyday
working class citizen doesn't have a choice in this arrangement. Some, or many,
of the items in the local grocery store will be enabled by modern day slavery.
We can all make better choices, but ultimately the choice of any one individual
will change very little, and it is a privilege to be particular about the
source of your basic needs. I think Jan's rationalization can be read as, 
.Dq isn't everybody exploiting somebody?
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
[Verse 2]
.Do Bobby wakes up at noon
Locks himself in his room
Used a belt on his back
Hits it hard, made it crack
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
Bobby wakes up late. I guess he was high last night. He locks himself in his
room, so he lives with other people, probably family. Alex uses a very graphic
image to describe Bobby's self hatred. Does Bobby hate himself for using? Or
does he use because he hates himself? I think it tends to be both. Alex uses
the word crack to describe the belt's power against his back, but it's probably
also a reference to crack cocaine.
.Pp
I notice that he locks himself in his room not to use, but rather to lash out
at himself. I wonder if Bobby has anyone he could talk to about his dependency.
If he did, would he feel able to? People talk about the
.Dq male loneliness epidemic
a lot these days. I find that many expressions of masculinity come with a lot
of shame about being vulnerable with others. A lot of men can't even comprehend
their own feelings, because they've never practised talking about them. A lot
of men would rather drown themselves in substance than be emotionally
vulnerable with another person. We don't know why Bobby uses, but it's clear
that he could use a friend.
.Pp
Alex opens the verse with Bobby waking up, and it makes me think his feelings
of self hatred are haunting him as soon as he wakes. He woke up late, and
likely used last night. I wonder if Jan's cold awakening happened at the same
time that he used.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
.Do He got high later on
Picks up Jan, drives her home
She don't talk but she knows
Why the boy's fever grows
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
Bobby gets high later on, after lashing out at himself. The order seems
relevant; like his first solution was the belt, and only after that didn't work
did he turn to substance. Like virtually all addicts, Bobby likely uses as an
escape. He picks up Jan, likely to buy from her again, then drops her off home.
Jan trusts Bobby enough to drive her around, so I figure this means they've
known each other a while, maybe even as friends. He shakes her hand during the
first deal, like they aren't close at all, but it's possible there's a time
skip. She doesn't say anything about his drug use, despite seeing that he is
getting worse.
.Pp
Alex uses the words
.Dq boy's fever
to describe Bobby's substance dependency. It's a physical description, so at
face value, this is about Bobby's physical condition getting worse. But to me,
boy's fever has connotations of a childhood illness. Has Jan started thinking
of Bobby like a younger brother? Further, if we think of his substance abuse as
a symptom, then his real fever is his mental health. In particular, Alex says
Jan knows why his fever is getting worse. It makes me think Bobby might be
distracting himself from something he can't control. Maybe Jan doesn't say
anything because there's nothing she can say to make it better.
.Bd -literal -decorate quote
[Chorus]
.Do Who are you?
I guess it's what we do
To be new
What a scheme
I guess that's what she means
In your dreams
.Dc
.Ed
.Pp
This time, I think the chorus is an exchange with Bobby, and Jan in Bobby's
dream. Jan asks him who he is, and I think the underlying question is
.Dq Why do you use?
Bobby replies, that it is what he does to be new. Vices are inherently used by
people with a desire to be changed. In the short-term, your mind is changed to
be slower, or more focused, or your negative emotions are silenced, or drowned
out by euphoria. I think Bobby uses for relief. But also, Bobby's drug use can
be seen as a form of self destruction. By destroying himself, his body and his
mind, he can tear away parts of himself, and become something new. This aligns
with the image of Bobby's self harm.
.Pp
Dream Jan replies, 
.Dq What a scheme.
I interpret this as Jan saying, sure that will work, but only briefly. 
But then
.Dq I guess that's what she means
sounds like Dream Jan didn't say it, and rather Bobby just figured that's what
her initial question means. So maybe the question is,
.Dq Who do you want to be? ,
and Bobby doesn't have an answer for that.
.Pp
I think it's significant, and maybe sad, that Bobby dreams about Jan, on an
apparently regular basis. Is Jan the closest thing to a friend for Bobby?
Bobby talks with her in his dreams, and she asks him about who he wants to be.
What does it say that Jan doesn't talk with him in person, so he dreams about
it instead? 
